<Docs
	page="recipes/swift"
	title="Swift"
	lede="GRDB, SQLite.swift, and PostgresNIO work out of the box."
>
	<section>
		<h2>Which strings squill rewrites</h2>
		<p>
			Multi-line string literals (<code>"""</code>) without interpolation.
			squill lays the query out one clause per line, with the closing{" "}
			<code>"""</code> at the statement&rsquo;s indent and every line of SQL
			at or past it, which is how Swift measures the string&rsquo;s
			indentation. Single-line strings are left exactly as written, and so
			is any string with a <code>\(&hellip;)</code> interpolation &mdash;
			SQL with holes in it isn&rsquo;t SQL squill can parse &mdash; or a
			backslash escape. Raw strings (<code>#"""</code>) aren&rsquo;t taken
			either.
		</p>
		<p>
			The indent character comes from the file, so a spaces-indented project
			stays spaces. squill&rsquo;s own indent width is 2; set{" "}
			<code>indent-width = 4</code> in the rule to match Xcode&rsquo;s.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>GRDB</h2>
		<p>
			The built-in <code>swift</code> grammar&rsquo;s default query takes any
			string passed as a <code>sql:</code> argument &mdash;{" "}
			<code>db.execute(sql:)</code>, <code>Row.fetchAll(db, sql:)</code>,
			and the rest of GRDB&rsquo;s API &mdash; and reads it as SQLite,
			whatever the configured dialect. No query file needed:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="swift-grdb" lang="swift" />
		<p>
			GRDB&rsquo;s <code>?</code> and <code>:name</code> arguments are
			SQLite&rsquo;s own, so they pass through as written.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>SQLite.swift</h2>
		<p>
			The default query also takes the first argument of{" "}
			<code>run</code>, <code>execute</code>, <code>prepare</code>, and{" "}
			<code>scalar</code>, in the configured dialect &mdash; so set the rule
			to SQLite:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="swift-sqlite" lang="swift" />
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>PostgresNIO and SQLKit</h2>
		<p>
			PostgresNIO&rsquo;s <code>query</code> and SQLKit&rsquo;s{" "}
			<code>raw</code> take their SQL first, too. Both bind values through
			string interpolation (<code>\(id)</code>), and a string with an
			interpolation is never touched, so squill formats the queries that
			take no values and leaves the rest as written:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="swift-postgresnio" lang="swift" />
	</section>
</Docs>
